#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "935911d1-cf0f-5abc-a7c2-a7d9ff328038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{- (t - 1)} - e^{-2(t - 1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228661} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5273d8-f946-50f9-a18f-883b358975c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 1)} - e^{-2(t - 1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228662} {:answer/id #uuid "c36ee300-02d3-50cd-ba93-46ec93dfed5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^{- (t - 1)} + e^{-2(t - 1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228663} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d7c3ae-2403-5826-8cd7-2daf80feaddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-t} - e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228664} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac7b55c-320e-58f7-827f-e74f22acf4ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 1)} - e^{- (t - 2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228665}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e1294a04-a4d7-549c-ab04-a206d685e72c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228660}] :question/id #uuid "81cfd049-da26-58a2-97d8-8fcbe6ff442d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342315" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$\\frac{dy}{dt} + y = u_{1}(t) \\cdot e^{-2(t - 1)}, y(0) = 1$,\nwhere $u_{1}(t) = u(t - 1)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ is\n$Y(s) = \\frac{1}{s + 1} + (e^{-s})/((s + 1)(s + 2))$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = e^{-t} + u(t - 1) \\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb0f356-7ea1-5211-98fe-5b84dd6ba27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb0f356-7ea1-5211-98fe-5b84dd6ba27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315294} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd5c0207-5c3d-553c-af70-1c15c3661a5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97923b3c-8f90-512a-b7ec-bb8a53b3d22c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342532" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+4y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2(t-2)^{3}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2u(t-2)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2((t+2)-2)^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)(t-2)^{3}} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
